    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-114743",
      "sequence_position": 20,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14190876/assets/e2a62b8616e1178cc7200c91e4e45366b5aece9d7e9838f02b9c60217fd79f8b.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y={f}^{′}(x)$, the derivative of $y=f(x)$, is shown above. Which of the following could be the graph of $y={f}^{″}(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, if we denote $g(x)={f}^{′}(x)$ then ${f}^{″}(x)={g}^{′}(x).$ So, we need to sketch the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$ given the graph of $y=g(x),$ as usual.\n\nNotice the following:\n\n- At the stationary points, where the tangent line to $y=g(x)$ is parallel to the $x$-axis, we have ${g}^{′}(x)=0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is increasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- When $g(x)$ is decreasing, we have ${g}^{′}(x)<0.$\n\nWe can now summarize the information from the graph in the table below. Note that the stationary point was identified (approximately) from the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)$:\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $g$ | $↘$ | $\\text{min}$ | $↗$ |\n| ${g}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ |\n\nAdditionally, from the graph of $y=g(x),$ we can conclude that there are no inflection points.  Also, the graph of $y=g(x)$ is concave up in $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ So, ${g}^{″}(x)>0$ and $y={g}^{′}(x)$ is increasing.\n\nTherefore, the graph of $y={g}^{′}(x)={f}^{″}(x)$ may look as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14190876/assets/2d8ab7a5804de834dd6640e1cb7ba9e8ec13c6e1d36ef5bb6b0115d8eaa00095.png)",
